e bill for new data centers blocked from passage\nL100: \nL101: “The Ratepayer Protection Act represents the most meaningful bipartisan step Congress could take to protect the American people from higher prices for electricity.”\nL102: \nL103: cite32†Image L104: \nL105: View Husted’s remarks cite33†here†www.youtube.com .\nL106: WASHINGTON – Sen. Jon Husted (R-Ohio) today brought his Ratepayer Protection Act to the Senate floor, seeking its passage by unanimous consent—a process that expedites consideration of legislation in the United States Senate.\nL107: \nL108: A Senate Democrat objected, blocking the bill from Senate passage.\nL109: \nL110: The Ratepayer Protection Act would ensure American families and small businesses aren’t left paying for the energy infrastructure needed to support new data centers.\nL111: Husted introduced the cite34†bill on July 16, 2026.\nL112: \nL113: The Ratepayer Protection Act passed the U.S. House of Representatives on Sept. 16, 2026, by an overwhelming, bipartisan vote of 417-3.\nL114: \nL115: Key excerpts include:\nL116: \nL117: “Mr. President, America is expected to build the equivalent of 1,000 major data centers during the next five years.\nL118: \nL119: “You heard that correct.\nL120: \nL121: “1,000 data centers within the next five years, with more than 2,000 projects currently being proposed.\nL122: \nL123: . . .\nL124: “And we need to protect American ratepayers from footing the bill.”\nL125: \nL126: . . .\nL127: \nL128: “So, as we look to build the future, we must follow some basic principles of fairness.\nL129: \nL130: “The company that is creating enormous new demands for electricity should pay the costs required to serve them.”\nL131: \nL132: . . .\nL133: \nL134: “Working families, small businesses and senior citizens should not open up their electric bills and discover the pain for the power infrastructure needed by some large tech company in the world or in their neighborhood.”\nL135: . . .\nL136: \nL137: “That is the principle behind the Ratepayer Protection Act.\nL138: \nL139: “When a very large electricity customer, such as the data center, connects to the grid, it can require investments in new generation transmission lines, substations and local distribution systems.\nL140: \nL141: “The Ratepayer Protection Act would require state utility regulators to consider standards, ensuring that large load customers pay the full, incremental cost of the infrastructure needed to serve them.”\nL142: \nL143: . . .\nL144: “In plain language, if you create the cost, you should pay the cost.”\nL145: \nL146: . . .\nL147: \nL148: “Data centers should only be located where communities want them.\nL149: \nL150: “They can bring billions of dollars in private investment, construction work for electricians, pipefitters, equipment operators and other skilled trades, as well as revenue for schools and local services.\nL151: \nL152: “But those benefits should not come with a hidden charge of electric bills for families, farmers and small businesses.”\nL153: \nL154: . . .\nL155: “When energy supply adequately meets demand, it helps put downward pressure on prices for consumers.”\nL156: \nL157: . . .\nL158: \nL159: “Helps people save money.\nL160: \nL161: “It’s what it does.\nL162: \nL163: “They can keep more of their paycheck for what they need to spend on for their priorities.”\nL164: \nL165: . . .\nL166: \nL167: “We can protect families and small businesses from unfair costs.\nL168: \nL169: “The Ratepayer Protection Act rests on a principle every American understands: pay your own way.\nL170: “This is how we make sure American families are not left footing the bill for energy infrastructure required by data centers.\nL171: \nL172: “Most importantly, it will help the American people keep more of what they earn.”\nL173: \nL174: . . .\nL175: \nL176: “Yesterday, this bill passed the House of Representatives by a nearly unanimous vote of 417 to 3.”\nL177: \nL178: . . .\nL179: \nL180: “So, the members of the House, they did their jobs.\nL181: \nL182: “They stood up for the American ratepayer.\nL183: \nL184: “And it’s time for the Senate to do the same.”\nL185: \nL186: . . .\nL187: “In a few moments, I’m going to ask my colleagues in the Senate to support the Ratepayer Protection Act.\nL188: \nL189: “Together, we can send this bill to the president’s desk for his signature.”\nL190: \nL191: . . .\nL192: \nL193: “The Ratepayer Protection Act represents the most meaningful bipartisan step Congress could take to protect the American people from higher prices for electricity.\nL194: \nL195: “And I’m here today because we can get this done.”\nL196: \nL197: . . .\nL198: \nL199: “I am proud to have led this effort in the United States Senate.\nL200: “And I’m going to urge unanimous consent for this commonsense bill.”\nL201: \nL202: View Husted’s full remarks cite33†here†www.youtube.com .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHeinrich Offers His GRID Savings Act to ... | Senator Martin Heinrich (https://www.heinrich.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/heinrich-offers-his-grid-savings-act-to-force-ai-data-centers-to-pay-for-grid-upgrades-highlights-how-husted-backed-bill-falls-short)\nciteturn972view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn971search3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Skip to content L1:   * cite1†About L2:     * cite1†About Martin L3:     * cite2†Committee Assignments L4:     * cite3†New Mexico L5:   * cite4†Services L6:     * cite5†Help with a Federal Agency L7:     * cite6†Health Care Resources L8:     * cite7†Student Resources L9:     * cite8†Internships L10:     * cite9†Veterans Resources L11:     * cite10†Visiting D.C. L12:     * cite11†Service Academy Nominations L13:     * cite12†Flag Requests L14:     * cite13†Federal Grant Funding L15:     * cite14†Mobile Office Hours L16:     * cite15†Appropriations Requests L17:     * cite16†Congressionally Directed Spending L18:     * cite17†Casework Success Stories L19:     * cite18†Medal Retrieval Assistance L20:     * cite19†Judicial Nominations L21:     * cite20†Congratulatory Letters L22:   * cite21†Priorities L23:     * cite22†Issues L24:     * cite23†Legislation L25:     * cite24†Voting Record L26:   * cite25†Newsroom L27:     * cite26†Press Releases L28:     * cite27†In the News L29:     * cite28†Martin's Blog L30:   * cite29†Resources L31:     * cite30†Solar Toolkit L32:     * cite31†Wildfire Preparedness L33:     * cite32†Kids To Parks L34:     * cite33†RECA Eligibility Information L35:   * cite34†Contact L36:     * cite35†Write Martin L37:     * cite36†Scheduling Requests L38:     * cite37†Newsletter Sign-up L39:     * cite5†Request Assistance L40:   * cite38†Español L41: Website Search Open\nL42: \nL43: [Input: Search] Submit Site Search Query\nL44: \nL45: Website Search\nL46: \nL47:   * cite39†Senator Heinrich Facebook†www.facebook.com L48:   * cite40†Senator Heinrich Twitter†twitter.com L49:   * cite41†Senator Heinrich Instagram†www.instagram.com L50:   *   *   * cite42†Senator Heinrich Youtube†www.youtube.com L51:   * cite43†Senator Heinrich Flickr†www.flickr.com L52: \nL53: cite44†Home Logo Link L54: \nL55:   1. cite25†Newsroom L56:   2. cite26†Press Releases L57: \nL58: Published: September 17th, 2026\nL59: # Heinrich Offers His GRID Savings Act to Force AI Data Centers to Pay for Grid Upgrades, Highlights How Husted-Backed Bill Falls Short\nL60: \nL61: WASHINGTON — Today, U.S. Senator Martin Heinrich (D-N.M.), Ranking Member of the U.S. Senate Energy and Natural Resources Committee, took to the Senate floor to object to Senate passage of the Ratepayer Protection Act, legislation offered by U.S. Senator Jon Husted (R-Ohio), and pass Heinrich's GRID Savings Act instead.\nL62: While the Husted-backed legislation relies on a voluntary framework and fails to adequately protect American families from AI data center-caused increases in energy costs, Heinrich's legislation forces large load customers—like data centers—to pay for the facilities needed to connect them to the grid. Unfortunately, U.S. Senator Bernie Moreno (R-Ohio) objected to Heinrich's request to pass the GRID Savings Act.\nL63: “When I open my electricity bill, and read to see how much I owe for the month, it doesn’t say I ‘may’ pay that amount. It doesn’t say I ‘should’ pay that amount. It’s not optional. Just like every other bill-paying American out there, I open that bill and I have to pay it. The same needs to be true for AI data centers,” said Heinrich. “If hyperscalers and other big tech companies need expensive new facilities and more energy, they should pay for it—not our hardworking families and small businesses.”\nL64: Heinrich continued, “The Senator from Ohio and I agree on that principle. And plenty of big tech companies have said that’s their plan. But it’s not enough to just take their word for it. And it’s not enough for us to just tell states to consider making data centers pay for grid upgrades. Rather than voluntary pledges or suggestions to states, Congress needs to pass real legislation with real teeth.”\nL65: cite45†VIDEO: U.S. Senator Martin Heinrich (D-N.M.), Ranking Member of the U.S. Senate Energy and Natural Resources Committee, urges his colleagues to pass his GRID Savings Act to force data centers to pay for their grid upgrades, rather than pass along those costs to families, September 17, 2026.†www.youtube.com L66: \nL67: A video of Heinrich’s floor speech can be found cite45†here†www.youtube.com .\nL68: \nL69: A transcript of Heinrich’s remarks as delivered is below:\nL70: \nL71: M. President, reserving the right to object—\nL72: The Senator from Ohio and I agree on one thing: AI data centers could mean higher costs for American families.\nL73: \nL74: And in a lot of places, they already do.\nL75: \nL76: The Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas found that data center demand has already pushed electricity prices across the nation up by two to six percent – a number that they expect to increase to 20 to 30 percent by 2028.\nL77: \nL78: In the mid-Atlantic region, that means an extra $840 a year per family in the next two years. $840.\nL79: We expect to see increases like this across the country as more AI data centers come online. But the Ratepayer Protection Act falls short of what we need to do to respond.\nL80: \nL81: When I open my mail and unfold my electricity bill, and read how much I owe for the month, it doesn’t say I “may” pay that amount.\nL82: \nL83: It doesn’t say I “should” pay that amount.\nL84: \nL85: It’s not optional.\nL86: \nL87: Just like every other bill-paying American family out there, I open that bill and I have to pay it.\nL88: The same needs to be true for AI data centers.\nL89: \nL90: If hyperscalers and other big tech developers need expensive new facilities and more energy, they should pay for it—not our hardworking families and small businesses.\nL91: \nL92: I think the Senator from Ohio and I agree on that principle. And plenty of big tech companies have said that’s their plan.\nL93: \nL94: But it’s not enough to just take their word for it.\nL95: \nL96: It’s not enough for us to tell states to consider making data centers pay for grid upgrades.\nL97: Rather than voluntary pledges or suggestions to states, Congress needs to pass real legislation with real teeth.\nL98: \nL99: That’s why I introduced the GRID Savings Act to require, not a voluntary agreement, large load customers—like data centers—to pay for the facilities needed to connect them to the grid.\nL100: \nL101: Not to consider it. Not to make pledges about it.\nL102: \nL103: To actually do it. To pay for the upgrades they need.\nL104: \nL105: That way, we can ensure reliable, low-cost energy to power our everyday lives.\nL106: ?My bill is what is needed to start to bring down energy prices.\nL107: \nL108: We also need to do more to get data centers to actually engage with communities from the beginning to the very end.\nL109: \nL110: We need to conserve local resources, especially water.\nL111: \nL112: And we need to protect communities from air pollution by using clean energy and battery storage.\nL113: \nL114: But we’re not going to get there with the bill the Senator from Ohio has proposed, and therefore, Mr. President, I object.\nL115: Mr. President, as if in legislative session, and notwithstanding, I would ask unanimous consent that the Committee on Energy and Natural Resources be discharged from further consideration of S.5199, the GRID Savings Act, and that the Senate proceed to its immediate consideration. Further, that the bill be considered read a third time and passed, and that the motion to reconsider be considered made and laid upon the table.\nL116: ###\nL117: \nL118:   * Print\nL119:   * Email\nL120:   * Share\nL121:   * Tweet\nL122: \nL123: cite46†Previous Article L124: \nL125: cite47†Next Article L126: \nL127:   * cite1†About L128:   * cite4†Helping You L129:   * cite21†Priorities L130:   * cite25†Newsroom L131:   * cite29†Resources L132:   * cite34†Contact L133:   * cite48†Privacy Policy L134: \nL135:   * cite39†Senator Heinrich Facebook†www.facebook.com L136:   * cite40†Senator Heinrich Twitter†twitter.com L137:   * cite41†Senator Heinrich Instagram†www.instagram.com L138:   *   *   * cite42†Senator Heinrich Youtube†www.youtube.com L139:   * cite43†Senator Heinrich Flickr†www.flickr.com L140: \nL141: cite1†About L142: cite1†About Martin cite2†Committee Assignments cite3†New Mexico L143: \nL144: cite4†Services L145: \nL146: cite5†Help with a Federal Agency cite6†Health Care Resources cite7†Student Resources cite8†Internships cite9†Veterans Resources cite10†Visiting D.C. cite11†Service Academy Nominations